05859375, -0.03277587890625, -0.0033130645751953125, -0.020751953125, -0.00600433349609375, 0.051300048828125, 0.0406494140625, 0.02874755859375, 0.0228424072265625, 0.01019287109375, 0.040252685546875, 0.0311279296875, -0.038482666015625, -0.002880096435546875, 0.00780487060546875, 0.0335693359375, 0.0019235610961914062, -0.01294708251953125, 0.0171661376953125, -0.01513671875, -0.0279541015625, 0.0262908935546875, -0.00011008977890014648, -9.322166442871094e-05, 0.0012369155883789062, -0.018890380859375, -0.007221221923828125, -0.0002913475036621094, -0.015716552734375, -0.022796630859375, 0.0163421630859375, -0.00855255126953125, 0.03533935546875, 0.005634307861328125, -0.030242919921875, -0.0171661376953125, -0.0022258758544921875, 0.01226043701171875, -0.006412506103515625, 0.01548004150390625, 0.0009341239929199219, -0.0247955322265625, 0.01021575927734375, -0.0237884521484375, -0.004611968994140625, 0.026824951171875, -0.01522064208984375, -0.01032257080078125, 0.0244903564453125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Recent research has begun to investigate the application of Geometric Algebra (GA) to the modeling, analysis, and control of dynamical systems, especially in the context of electrical circuits and related engineering domains. For example, Hitzer [1] applied GA to circuit variables and mesh analysis; Hitzer [2] extended linear systems and control theory to Clifford (geometric) algebras, focusing on grade-homogeneous signals; and Li & Klose [3] developed multivector approaches to engineering design. Geometric Algebra itself is a mathematical framework based on a real vector space of dimension \\( n = p+q \\) with a metric of signature \\((p, q)\\): \\(p\\) basis vectors square to \\(+1\\), \\(q\\) to \\(-1\\).\n\nA *blade* is constructed as a wedge (exterior) product of \\( r \\) orthonormal basis vectors, with *grade* \\( r \\). General elements\u2014*multivectors*\u2014are linear combinations of blades of different grades (e.g., scalars, vectors, bivectors, etc.). Each multivector \\( M \\) can be uniquely decomposed as \\( M = \\sum_{r=0}^n \\langle M \\rangle_r \\), where \\( \\langle M \\rangle_r \\) denotes the projection to grade \\( r \\) (i.e., the grade-\\( r \\) part). An induced norm, such as the Euclidean norm \\(\\|M\\| := \\left( \\sum_{r=0}^n \\| \\langle M \\rangle_r \\|_2^2 \\right)^{1/2}\\), can be used to measure multivector \"size\" where \\( \\| \\cdot \\|_2 \\) is the usual Euclidean norm on each grade-\\( r \\) component.\n\n---\n\n### Motivation\n\nThe Laplace transform is central to systems analysis, converting time-domain ODEs into algebraic equations for frequency-domain reasoning (e.g., stability, resonance, feedback, and controller synthesis). In many advanced applications\u2014such as multi-phase power circuits, spatial robotics, and coupled electromagnetic systems\u2014physical models are elegantly and naturally expressed using geometric algebra, wherein states and signals are multivector-valued and may mix grades. This leads to noncommutative algebraic combinations, complicating both analysis and computation.\n\nDespite this, standard Laplace analysis is unavailable for such settings: existing transformations are limited to scalar, complex, and select hypercomplex signals, forcing practitioners to decompose multivector signals into components and apply transforms separately\u2014an approach that is cumbersome and often obscures the underlying geometric or physical structure. Notably, a unified GA model capturing, say, both currents (vectors) and magnetic flux (bivectors) cannot be directly treated in the Laplace domain without an appropriate extension. This limitation restricts the application of powerful frequency-domain tools in contemporary system design.\n\n---\n\n### Literature Review\n\nEfforts to generalize the Laplace transform beyond scalars include:\n\n- Hitzer [1]: Applied GA for static and dynamic circuit analysis, but did not develop a multivector-valued operational calculus.\n- Hitzer [2]: Extended system and control theory to Clifford-valued dynamics, focusing on grade-homogeneous signals and largely excluding mixed-grade or noncommutative cases.\n- Li & Klose [3]: Presented multivector methods in engineering, with an emphasis on geometric synthesis and static relations; dynamic transforms were not addressed.\n- B\u00fclow & Sommer [4]: Extended Laplace transforms to quaternionic signals, but only in special cases since quaternionic noncommutativity complicates the method and excludes general mixed-grade algebras.\n- Brackx et al. [5]: Investigated Laplace and Fourier transforms for commutative hypercomplex algebras (e.g., split-complex numbers), but techniques do not extend to noncommutative or mixed-grade GA-valued functions.\n\nIn summary, a rigorous, broadly applicable Laplace transform framework for arbitrary (possibly mixed-grade and noncommutative) multivector-valued functions in \\(\\mathcal{G}_{p,q}\\) is lacking, especially for applications encountered in modern engineering models.\n\n---\n\n### Research Gap and Objectives\n\n**Research Gap:**  \nThere is no general framework for the Laplace transform acting on arbitrary multivector-valued functions\u2014that is, functions of time with values in the full GA (potentially of mixed grades and involving noncommutative products). This omission precludes direct frequency-domain treatment of many modern physical systems best expressed in GA formalism.\n\n**Objectives:**  \n1. Provide a formal, general definition of the Laplace transform for arbitrary multivector-valued functions \\( M(t): \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathcal{G}_{p,q} \\).\n2. Demonstrate that fundamental Laplace properties (linearity, differentiation, invertibility) hold grade by grade, with careful attention to noncommutative aspects of the algebra.\n3. Use explicit examples to show that the framework enables frequency-domain engineering analysis for systems inaccessible to conventional Laplace or hypercomplex techniques.\n\n**Scope Restriction:**  \nTo ensure practical tractability, we present explicit computations and worked examples for signatures with \\( n \\leq 5 \\) (i.e., algebras of dimension at most 32). While general definitions and theorems apply for arbitrary \\( n \\), direct computation and visualization become unwieldy for larger systems; nonetheless, the generality of the framework remains intact, and higher-dimensional cases could in principle be addressed with symbolic computation or tailored numerical methods.\n\n---\n\n### Technical Background\n\n#### Multivector Functions and Notation\n\nLet \\( \\mathcal{G}_{p, q} \\) be a real geometric algebra of signature \\((p, q)\\) and dimension \\( 2^n \\). Let \\(\\langle \\cdot \\rangle_r\\) denote the grade-\\( r \\) projection, and define the induced norm for multivectors as\n\\[\n\\| M \\| := \\left( \\sum_{r=0}^n \\| \\langle M \\rangle_r \\|_2^2 \\right)^{1/2},\n\\]\nwhere \\( \\|\\cdot\\|_2 \\) is the Euclidean norm on the space of grade-\\( r \\) components.\n\nWe consider multivector-valued functions \\( M(t): [0, \\infty) \\to \\mathcal{G}_{p, q} \\) that are piecewise continuous and absolutely integrable: for all \\(T > 0\\), \\( \\int_0^T \\|M(t)\\|\\,dt < \\infty \\).\n\n---\n\n### Laplace Transform for Multivector-Valued Functions\n\n**Definition:**  \nGiven \\( M(t) = \\sum_{r=0}^n \\langle M(t) \\rangle_r \\), define the Laplace transform by\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}\\{M\\}(s) := \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} M(t)\\,dt = \\sum_{r=0}^n \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} \\langle M(t) \\rangle_r \\,dt,\n\\]\nwhere \\( e^{-st} \\) is scalar and commutes with all GA elements. Thus, integration acts independently on each grade, and cross-grade terms do not appear within the transform.\n\n**Invertibility:**  \nSince each grade component is integrated as a scalar-valued function, the classical Laplace inversion applies grade by grade. Provided each \\( \\langle M(t) \\rangle_r \\) meets standard scalar Laplace invertibility requirements (piecewise continuity, absolute integrability), the full multivector Laplace transform is invertible. This relies on the absence of cross-grade mixing within the transform; cf. Brackx et al. [5, Sec. 4] for hypercomplex cases, and note that the GA's noncommutative multiplication is not involved during Laplace integration itself.\n\n---\n\n### Main Properties and Examples\n\n**Theorem 1 (Linearity):**  \nFor multivector functions \\( M(t), N(t): [0, \\infty) \\to \\mathcal{G}_{p, q} \\) and scalars \\( a, b \\in \\mathbb{R} \\),\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}\\{a M(t) + b N(t)\\}(s) = a \\mathcal{L}\\{M(t)\\}(s) + b \\mathcal{L}\\{N(t)\\}(s),\n\\]\nacting independently on each grade.\n\n**Theorem 2 (Differentiation):**  \nSuppose \\( M(t) \\) is differentiable and both \\( M(t) \\) and \\( M'(t) \\) are absolutely integrable:\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{\\frac{dM}{dt}(t)\\right\\}(s) = s \\mathcal{L}\\{M(t)\\}(s) - M(0),\n\\]\nwith \\( s \\) scalar and commuting with all GA elements. This result, proved for each grade component, parallels the classical Laplace differentiation theorem.\n\n**Illustrative Example:**  \nLet \\( \\mathcal{G}_{3,0} \\) be the geometric algebra of dimension 8 (three-dimensional Euclidean space). Consider the function\n\\[\nM(t) = \\mathbf{e}_1 f_1(t) + (\\mathbf{e}_2 \\wedge \\mathbf{e}_3) f_2(t),\n\\]\nwhere \\( \\mathbf{e}_1 \\) is a vector, \\( \\mathbf{e}_2 \\wedge \\mathbf{e}_3 \\) a bivector, and \\( f_1"}}